A Formal Theory of Multiplanetary Species

A Theoretical Dissertation

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

We develop a rigorous mathematical theory of multiplanetary species grounded in viability theory, robust control barrier functions, and Leontief input–output analysis. The central object is an augmented state vector $z_M = (x_M, S_M, C_{M,\mathrm{local}})$ comprising industrial capital, physical reserves, and local production capacities of an extraterrestrial node. We introduce a Leontief bottleneck metric for exogenous dependency, a composite viability barrier that eliminates the circularity between viability and closure constraints, and a bounded recoverability barrier defined via the differential game of reachability to the nominal domain. The principal theorem establishes, under a $\mathcal{C}^1$ regularity hypothesis on the viability boundary, the chain of implications
\text{(Global robust CBF)} \implies \text{(Robust Nagumo–Aubin)} \overset{(A_6)}{\iff} \text{(Boundary dissipation)}.


We further establish a finite-time collapse bound under buffer exhaustion and characterize regenerative multiplanetary status as the robust controlled invariance of the recoverable viability kernel under complete critical decoupling from Earth. The theory is formalized in Lean 4, providing machine-verified proofs of the main equivalence.

---

1. Introduction

The dominant paradigm equates multiplanetary status with permanent human presence beyond Earth. This dissertation argues that such presence is neither necessary nor sufficient. A civilization becomes functionally multiplanetary when at least one extraterrestrial node achieves complete closure of its critical dependencies, maintaining viability and reconstruction capacity under worst-case perturbations without exogenous assistance.

The mathematical framework synthesizes three traditions. Viability theory (Nagumo 1942; Aubin 1991) characterizes controlled invariance of a closed set in terms of tangential conditions on its boundary. Robust control barrier functions (Ames, Tabuada, and collaborators) reformulate these tangency conditions as scalar dissipation inequalities with worst-case disturbance bounds. Leontief input–output analysis provides the appropriate metric for critical dependency: in a Leontief production function, inputs are perfectly complementary, so a single unavailable essential input can render an entire vital function inoperative, regardless of abundance in all other inputs.

The synthesis yields a theory that is mathematically tight, operationalizable, and machine-verifiable.

