3. Dependency Metrics and Critical Closure

3.1 Leontief Bottleneck Metric

For each $f_j \in \mathcal{F}_C$, let $R_j^{\mathrm{ess}} \subseteq R$ be the set of essential, non-substitutable inputs. The Leontief dependency of $f_j$ on exogenous supply is governed by the most binding essential input:
d_j(t) = \max_{r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}} \left( 1 - \frac{C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) + \tilde{S}_M(r,t)/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)}{N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t)} \right)_+,
\tag{3.1}


where $T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r) > 0$ is the characteristic regeneration time and $\tilde{S}_M$ is the filtered buffer capacity defined below. The maximal degree of critical dependency is
D_{\max}(t) = \mathcal{D}(z_M(t)) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{F}_C} d_j(t) \in [0,1].

Proposition 3.1 (Non-negativity of $\mathcal{D}$). For every $z_M \in \mathcal{Z}_M$, $\mathcal{D}(z_M) \in [0,1]$. Moreover, $\mathcal{D}(z_M) = 0$ if and only if for all $j \in \mathcal{F}_C$ and all $r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}$, $C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) + \tilde{S}_M(r,t)/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r) \ge N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t)$.

Proof. Immediate from the definition (3.1) and the positive-part operator. $\square$

3.2 Transient Buffer Filtering

The steady-state approximation $\tilde{S}_M \approx S_M$ is valid only for $t \gg T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)$. In transient regimes, the effective buffer is
\tilde{S}_M(r,t) = \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} e^{-(t-s)/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} S_M(r,s)\, ds.
\tag{3.2}


This convolutional filter satisfies:

(i) $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) \ge 0$ whenever $S_M(r,s) \ge 0$ for $s \le t$.

(ii) If $S_M(r,s) > 0$ on a set of positive measure in $(-\infty,t]$, then $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) > 0$.

(iii) In steady state $S_M(r,s) \equiv S_M^*$, $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) \to S_M^*$.

Convention 3.2. Throughout, we use $\tilde{S}_M$ in the dependency metric (3.1) but the raw stock $S_M$ in the buffer resistance functional (3.4). This asymmetry is deliberate: $\tilde{S}_M$ governs the instantaneous dependency ratio, while $S_M$ governs the physically extractable reserve.

3.3 Critical Decoupling

Definition 3.3 (Critical Decoupling). The node $v_M$ is critically decoupled from Earth at time $t_0$ if
u_{E \to M}^{\mathrm{crit}}(t) \equiv \mathbf{0}, \quad \forall t \ge t_0,


where $u_{E \to M}^{\mathrm{crit}}$ denotes the tensor of exogenous flows essential to maintaining at least one function $f_j \in \mathcal{F}_C$. Non-critical flows—cultural, scientific, passive telemetry—may remain positive without compromising structural autonomy.

Lemma 3.4 (Non-criticality certificate). A flow $u^{\mathrm{non-crit}}$ is strictly non-essential if for all $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ and all trajectories of (2.1) under $\pi^*$, the projection $\mathcal{D}(z_M(t))$ is unchanged when $u^{\mathrm{non-crit}} \equiv 0$. Formally:
\sup_{t \ge t_0} \left\| \frac{\partial \mathcal{D}(z_M(t))}{\partial u^{\mathrm{non-crit}}} \right\| = 0.


If this condition fails for any $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, the flow is reclassified into $u^{\mathrm{crit}}$.

3.4 Dynamic Buffer Resistance

Definition 3.5 (Buffer resistance time). Under the worst-case admissible perturbation,
T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M) = \inf_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}} \inf \left\{ T \ge 0 \;\middle|\; \exists r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}},\ S_M(r,0) + \int_0^T \bigl( C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) + \rho_M(r,t) - N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t) \bigr)\, dt < 0 \right\}.
\tag{3.3}

Proposition 3.6. If for some $r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}$, $N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t) > C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) + \rho_M(r,t)$ for all $t \in [0,T]$ and the deficit is uniformly bounded below, then $T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M) \le S_M(r,0)/\inf_{[0,T]}(N_{\mathrm{requis}} - C_{M,\mathrm{local}} - \rho_M)$.

Proof. Immediate from (3.3) by comparison. $\square$

