5. Robust Controlled Invariance

5.1 Bouligand Tangent Cone

For a closed set $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ and $z \in K$,
\mathcal{T}_K(z) = \left\{ v \in \mathbb{R}^N \;\middle|\; \liminf_{t \to 0^+} \frac{\operatorname{dist}(z + tv, K)}{t} = 0 \right\}.
\tag{5.1}


This is the Bouligand (or contingent) cone, closed and non-empty.

5.2 Smooth Characterization Under $(A_6)$

Lemma 5.1 (Smooth boundary characterization). Let $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ be closed with non-empty interior, and suppose $\partial K$ is a $\mathcal{C}^1$ manifold. Let $h : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}$ be a $\mathcal{C}^1$ regular defining function for $K$, i.e., $K = \{h \ge 0\}$ and $\nabla h(z) \neq 0$ for all $z \in \partial K$. Then for all $z \in \partial K$,
\mathcal{T}_K(z) = \left\{ v \in \mathbb{R}^N \;\middle|\; \langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle \ge 0 \right\}.
\tag{5.2}

Proof. Let $z \in \partial K$.

(⊇) Let $v \in \mathbb{R}^N$ with $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle > 0$. By $\mathcal{C}^1$ regularity, $h(z + tv) = h(z) + t\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle + o(t) > 0$ for small $t > 0$. Thus $z + tv \in K$, and $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$.

If $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle = 0$, choose $u \in \mathbb{R}^N$ with $\langle \nabla h(z), u \rangle > 0$, and set $v_n = v + u/n$. Then $\langle \nabla h(z), v_n \rangle > 0$, so $v_n \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$. Since $\mathcal{T}_K(z)$ is closed and $v_n \to v$, we obtain $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$.

(⊆) Let $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$. By definition, there exist sequences $z_n \in K$ with $z_n \to z$ and $t_n \to 0^+$ such that $t_n(z_n - z) \to v$. Since $h(z_n) \ge 0$ and $h(z) = 0$, Taylor's theorem gives
0 \le h(z_n) = \langle \nabla h(z), z_n - z \rangle + o(|z_n - z|).


Multiplying by $t_n > 0$:
0 \le \langle \nabla h(z), t_n(z_n - z) \rangle + t_n \cdot o(|z_n - z|).


As $n \to \infty$: $t_n(z_n - z) \to v$, so the first term tends to $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle$. The second term tends to $0$ because $t_n \cdot o(|z_n - z|) = o(|t_n(z_n - z)|) \to 0$. Hence $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle \ge 0$. $\square$

5.3 Robust Nagumo–Aubin Condition

Definition 5.2 (Robust Nagumo–Aubin). A policy $\pi : \mathcal{Z}_M \to \mathcal{U}_M$ achieves robust controlled invariance of $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ if
\forall z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}, \quad F_\sigma\bigl(z, \pi(z), d\bigr) \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z).
\tag{5.3}


Note the strict universal quantification over $d$; the naive existential form $F(z, \pi(z), \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}) \cap \mathcal{T}_K(z) \neq \emptyset$ is not robust and is excluded.

5.4 Global Robust CBF Condition

Definition 5.3 (Global robust CBF). A policy $\pi$ and a class-$\mathcal{K}_\infty$ function $\alpha$ satisfy the global robust CBF condition for $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+$ if
\forall z \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}, \quad \bigl\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z),\, F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \bigr\rangle \ge -\alpha\bigl(h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z)\bigr).
\tag{5.4}

