6. Main Equivalence Theorem

6.1 Hypotheses

(A₁) The hybrid vector field $F_\sigma : \mathcal{Z}_M \times \mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}} \to \mathbb{R}^N$ is locally Lipschitz in $z_M$ and continuous on $\mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$.

(A₂) $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is compact with $\operatorname{Int}(\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+) \neq \emptyset$.

(A₃) $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ is compact, given by (2.2).

(A₄) If $\mathcal{D}(z_M) > \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$, then $T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M) < \infty$.

(A₅) $\mathcal{U}_M \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is compact.

(A₆) $\partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is a $\mathcal{C}^1$ manifold and $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+$ is a regular defining function on $\partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$: $\nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z) \neq 0$ for all $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$.

6.2 Statement

Theorem 6.1 (Robust Nagumo–Aubin / CBF equivalence). Under (A₁)–(A₆) and the critical decoupling condition $u_{E \to M}^{\mathrm{crit}} \equiv \mathbf{0}$, the following three conditions are related by:
\boxed{\ \text{(1)} \implies \text{(2)} \overset{(A_6)}{\iff} \text{(3)}\ }


where:

(1) Global robust CBF satisfaction. There exists $\alpha \in \mathcal{K}_\infty$ such that (5.4) holds.

(2) Robust Nagumo–Aubin controlled invariance. (5.3) holds.

(3) Boundary dissipation.
\forall z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}, \quad \bigl\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z),\, F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \bigr\rangle \ge 0.
\tag{6.1}

6.3 Proof

Step 1: $(1) \implies (3)$. Let $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$. By definition of $\partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$, $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z) = 0$. Since $\alpha \in \mathcal{K}_\infty$, $\alpha(0) = 0$. Inequality (5.4) becomes
\bigl\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \bigr\rangle \ge 0


for all $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, which is exactly (6.1). $\square$

Step 2: $(3) \iff (2)$ under (A₆). By Lemma 5.1, for $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$,
\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z) = \left\{ v \;\middle|\; \langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), v \rangle \ge 0 \right\}.


Thus for any $w \in \mathbb{R}^N$ and $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$:
w \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z) \iff \langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), w \rangle \ge 0.


Applying $w = F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d)$ for arbitrary $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, condition (2) is equivalent to (3). $\square$

Step 3: $(1) \implies (2)$. Compose Steps 1 and 2. $\square$

Corollary 6.2. Under (A₁)–(A₆), $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is robustly positively invariant under $\pi$:
\forall z_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}},\ \forall t \ge 0, \quad z_M(t; z_0, \pi, d) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+.

Proof. By (A₁), trajectories are uniquely defined. By (1) ⟹ (2) and the classical Nagumo–Aubin theorem (Aubin 1991, Thm. 4.3.1), tangency on $\partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ ensures that trajectories starting in $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ never exit. $\square$

6.4 Collapse Condition

Theorem 6.3 (Finite-time collapse under critical dependency breach). Under (A₁)–(A₅), if $D_{\max}(t_0) > \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$ and $T_{\mathrm{closure}} > T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M(t_0))$, then there exists $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ such that
z_M\bigl(t_0 + T_{\mathrm{collapse}}; z_M(t_0), \pi, d\bigr) \notin \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+, \quad T_{\mathrm{collapse}} \le T_{\mathrm{buffer}}\bigl(z_M(t_0)\bigr) < \infty.

Proof. By (A₄), $T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M(t_0)) < \infty$. Choose $d^* \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ realizing the worst-case depletion rate in (3.3). At time $T_{\mathrm{buffer}}$, some $r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}$ satisfies $S_M(r, T_{\mathrm{buffer}}) < 0$ along this trajectory, which contradicts $S_M \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|R|}$. Hence the trajectory leaves $\mathcal{Z}_M$, and in particular $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_M$. $\square$

