7. Recoverability and Regeneration

7.1 Robust Recovery

Definition 7.1 (Robust recoverability). For $z_k \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ and $\tau \ge 0$,
\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau) = 1 \iff \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}},\ \exists T \le \tau: z_M(t_k + T; z_k, \pi^*, d) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{nominal}}^+.

Proposition 7.2. If $z_k \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ and (4.2) attains its infimum, then $\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau^*) = 1$ with $\tau^* = \tau_{\max} - h_{\mathrm{rec}}(z_k) \le \tau_{\max}$.

Proof. By Definition 4.3, $h_{\mathrm{rec}}(z_k) \ge 0$, so $\tau^* \ge 0$. By (4.2), the inner inf-sup value satisfies $\inf_\pi \sup_d (\cdot) \le \tau_{\max} + \kappa$. When the infimum over $\pi$ is attained at $\pi^*$, the corresponding sup over $d$ is $\le \tau_{\max} - h_{\mathrm{rec}}(z_k) = \tau^*$. Hence for every $d$, the trajectory reaches $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{nominal}}^+$ within $\tau^*$. $\square$

7.2 Stochastic Recovery

Under a probability measure $\mathbb{P}_{\mathcal{D}}$ on the Borel $\sigma$-algebra of $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$-valued trajectories,
\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{stoch}}(\tau) = \mathbb{P}_{\mathcal{D}}\left( \inf\{t \ge 0 : z_M(t_k + t) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{nominal}}^+\} \le \tau \;\middle|\; z_M(t_k) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+,\ u^{\mathrm{crit}} \equiv \mathbf{0} \right).


The deterministic bound $\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau^*) = 1$ implies $\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{stoch}}(\tau^*) = 1$ for any $\mathbb{P}_{\mathcal{D}}$ supported on $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$.

7.3 Regenerative Criterion

Definition 7.3 (Extinction predicate). Let $\mathcal{G}(t) = (\mathcal{V}(t), \mathcal{E}(t))$ be the civilizational graph with at least two nodes $v_E$ (Earth) and $v_M$ (Mars). Then
\text{Extinction}(v_E) \iff \forall t \ge t_E,\ x_E(t) \notin \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable},E}^+.

Definition 7.4 (Regenerative multiplanetary species). Humanity is a regenerative multiplanetary species if, under the residual decoupling conditions
\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial x_E} \equiv \mathbf{0} \quad \text{on } \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+, \qquad u_{v_E \to v_M}^{\mathrm{crit}}(t) \equiv \mathbf{0} \quad \forall t \ge t_E,
\tag{7.1}


the following holds for every extinction event:
\boxed{\ \left( \text{Extinction}(v_E) \land z_M(t_E^-) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+ \right) \implies z_M(t_E^+) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+.\ }
\tag{7.2}

Proposition 7.5 (Sufficient condition for regenerative status). Under (7.1), if the state is continuous at $t_E$ (i.e., $z_M(t_E^+) = z_M(t_E^-)$), then (7.2) holds trivially.

Proof. By continuity, $z_M(t_E^+) = z_M(t_E^-) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$. $\square$

Corollary 7.6 (Persistence of recoverability). Under (7.1) and Proposition 7.5, if in addition $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ is robustly controlled-invariant under $\pi^*$ and $u^{\mathrm{crit}} \equiv \mathbf{0}$, then
\forall t \ge t_E,\quad z_M(t) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+.

Proof. By Proposition 7.5, $z_M(t_E) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$. By robust controlled invariance of $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, the trajectory remains in $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ for all $t \ge t_E$. $\square$

