9. The Multiplanetary Criterion

Theorem 9.1 (Characterization of multiplanetary status). Humanity is a regenerative multiplanetary species in the sense of Definition 7.4 if and only if there exist a policy $\pi^*$ and a class-$\mathcal{K}_\infty$ function $\alpha$ such that:

(I) Uniform sub-threshold closure:
\sup_{t \ge t_0} D_{\max}(t) \le \delta < \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}.

(II) Augmented robust invariance:
\forall z \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}, \quad \bigl\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), F_\sigma(z, \pi^*(z), d) \bigr\rangle \ge -\alpha(h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z)).

(III) Robust self-recovery:
\forall z_k \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+,\quad \mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau^*) = 1.

Proof. The equivalence follows from Definition 7.4, Theorem 6.1 (which turns (II) into controlled invariance of $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$), Proposition 7.2 (which turns (III) into finite-time reachability of $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{nominal}}^+$), and Corollary 7.6 (which extends the property to all $t \ge t_E$). Conditions (I)–(III) are exactly the properties required so that the loss of Earth leaves Mars within its recoverable kernel and able to autonomously regenerate. $\square$

Interpretation. Condition (I) eliminates critical dependencies; (II) guarantees that these dependencies remain eliminated under worst-case perturbations; (III) ensures that any post-catastrophic degraded state is recoverable to nominal operation within finite time, without exogenous assistance. Together, they define the precise threshold at which a civilization ceases to be a single-planet species with an outpost and becomes a genuinely multiplanetary one.

