10. Conclusion

We have developed a mathematical theory of multiplanetary species that is rigorous, operationalizable, and machine-verified. The key mathematical contributions are:

1. An augmented state space $(x_M, S_M, C_{M,\mathrm{local}})$ whose dynamics are coupled through local production and buffer depletion.
2. A Leontief bottleneck metric $\mathcal{D}(z_M)$ for critical dependency that captures non-substitutability of essential inputs.
3. A composite viability barrier $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+ = \min(h_{\mathrm{via}}, \delta_{\mathrm{crit}} - \mathcal{D})$ that eliminates the circularity between viability and closure constraints.
4. A bounded recoverability barrier $h_{\mathrm{rec}}$, defined intrinsically via the differential game of reachability, free of circular dependency on the recoverable set.
5. A main equivalence theorem relating global robust CBF satisfaction, Nagumo–Aubin controlled invariance, and boundary dissipation, with the nontrivial implication proved under explicit smoothness hypotheses.
6. A finite-time collapse bound $T_{\mathrm{collapse}} \le T_{\mathrm{buffer}}$, establishing that exceeding the critical dependency threshold forces exit from the viability domain within a computable horizon.
7. A regenerative multiplanetary criterion in which the collapse of the Earth node leaves the extraterrestrial node within its recoverable viability kernel, ensuring autonomous reconstruction to nominal operation.

The formalization in Lean 4 provides machine-verified proofs of the main equivalence, ensuring the absence of gaps in the analytic reasoning. The framework is deliberately minimal: it assumes only Lipschitz continuity of the vector field, compactness of the constraint and control sets, and $\mathcal{C}^1$ smoothness of the viability boundary. Under these hypotheses, the theory is complete and the conclusions are unconditional.

The implications extend beyond astrodynamics. The framework characterizes any critical infrastructure system whose viability depends on a set of non-substitutable inputs, whether those inputs are physical, informational, algorithmic, or institutional. The multiplanetary criterion is a special case of a general theory of robust civilizational viability under decoupling.

The aspiration to become multiplanetary is, mathematically, the aspiration to build a system whose viability kernel contains a recoverable subkernel that survives the loss of its parent node. The present theory specifies precisely what that requires.


