Appendix A. Existence and Uniqueness for the Hybrid System

A.1 Continuous Dynamics

Lemma A.1. Let $F : \mathcal{Z}_M \times \mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}} \to \mathbb{R}^N$ be locally Lipschitz in $z$, uniformly in $(u,d) \in \mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, and continuous on $\mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$. Let $u : [0,\infty) \to \mathcal{U}_M$ and $d : [0,\infty) \to \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ be measurable. Then for every $z_0 \in \mathcal{Z}_M$, the initial value problem
\dot{z}(t) = F(z(t), u(t), d(t)), \quad z(0) = z_0,


admits a unique maximal solution $z(\cdot; z_0, u, d)$ defined on some interval $[0, T_{\max})$, and either $T_{\max} = \infty$ or $\|z(t)\| \to \infty$ as $t \to T_{\max}^-$.

Proof. Since $F$ is locally Lipschitz in $z$ uniformly in $(u,d)$ and $\mathcal{U}_M \times \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ is compact, the map $t \mapsto F(z, u(t), d(t))$ is measurable in $t$ and continuous in $z$. By the Carathéodory–Lipschitz theorem (Coddington–Levinson, Thm. 1.1), for each $z_0$ there exists a unique maximal solution on $[0, T_{\max})$. The alternatives at $T_{\max}$ follow from the standard continuation criterion. $\square$

A.2 Hybrid Execution

Lemma A.2 (Well-posedness of hybrid trajectories). Under the continuity condition (2.3) on $\mathcal{S}$, and assuming:

· (i) $\mathcal{S}$ has empty interior;
· (ii) the dwell-time condition $\inf\{t_{k+1} - t_k\} \geq \tau_{\mathrm{dwell}} > 0$ holds for consecutive switching times $\{t_k\}$;
· (iii) $F_\sigma$ satisfies Lemma A.1 for each $\sigma$;

every initial condition $z_0 \in \mathcal{Z}_M$ admits a unique complete hybrid trajectory.

Proof. Between switches, trajectories are given by Lemma A.1. At each switching time $t_k$, the state remains continuous: $z(t_k^+) = z(t_k^-)$. The dwell-time hypothesis ensures finitely many switches in any compact interval, so Zeno phenomena are excluded. By induction on the sequence $\{t_k\}$, the hybrid trajectory extends to $[0,\infty)$. Uniqueness follows from Lemma A.1 and continuity of the reset map. $\square$

