Appendix C. Convolutional Buffer Filter Properties

C.1 Well-Definedness and Boundedness

Lemma C.1. Let $S_M(r,\cdot) \in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb{R})$. Then the convolutional filter
\tilde{S}_M(r,t) = \int_{-\infty}^t \frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} e^{-(t-s)/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} S_M(r,s)\, ds


is well-defined for all $t \in \mathbb{R}$, and $\tilde{S}_M(r,\cdot) \in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb{R})$.

Proof. Set $K_r(s) = T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)^{-1} e^{-s/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} \mathbf{1}_{s \geq 0}$. Then $\|K_r\|_{L^1} = 1$, so for $t$ in a compact interval $[a,b]$,
|\tilde{S}_M(r,t)| \leq \|K_r\|_{L^1} \cdot \|S_M(r,\cdot)\|_{L^\infty([a-T, b])} < \infty


for $T$ large enough. $\square$

C.2 Positivity Preservation

Lemma C.2. If $S_M(r,s) > 0$ on a set of positive Lebesgue measure in $(-\infty,t]$, then $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) > 0$.

Proof. $K_r > 0$ everywhere on $(0,\infty)$. The integral of a strictly positive integrand over a set of positive measure is strictly positive. $\square$

C.3 Steady-State Convergence

Lemma C.3. If $S_M(r,s) \to S_M^*$ as $s \to -\infty$, then $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) \to S_M^*$ as $t \to \infty$.

Proof. Change variables $u = t - s$:
\tilde{S}_M(r,t) = \int_0^\infty \frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} e^{-u/T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} S_M(r,t-u)\, du.


This is a weighted average of $S_M$ over a memory window of characteristic width $T_{\mathrm{rec}}$. As $t \to \infty$, $S_M(r,t-u) \to S_M^*$ uniformly for $u$ in any compact window. Splitting the integral at $u = M$ and using $\int_M^\infty K_r(u)\, du \to 0$, we obtain convergence. $\square$

C.4 Consistency with the Steady-State Metric

Lemma C.4. In steady state $S_M(r,s) \equiv S_M^*$, $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) = S_M^*$ for all $t$.

Proof. Direct computation: $\int_0^\infty K_r(u)\, du = 1$, so $\tilde{S}_M(r,t) = S_M^* \cdot 1 = S_M^*$. $\square$

This justifies the use of $\tilde{S}_M/T_{\mathrm{rec}}$ in the steady-state metric (3.1) and its agreement with $S_M/T_{\mathrm{rec}}$ when buffers are constant.

