Appendix D. Smooth Characterization of the Bouligand Tangent Cone

D.1 Setup

Let $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ be closed with $\operatorname{Int}(K) \neq \emptyset$. Let $h : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}$ be $\mathcal{C}^1$, and assume:
K = \{z \in \mathbb{R}^N : h(z) \geq 0\}, \qquad \nabla h(z) \neq 0 \ \forall z \in \partial K.

Recall the Bouligand (contingent) cone
\mathcal{T}_K(z) = \left\{ v \in \mathbb{R}^N : \liminf_{t \to 0^+} \frac{\operatorname{dist}(z + tv, K)}{t} = 0 \right\}.

D.2 Proof of Lemma 5.1

Lemma D.1 (= Lemma 5.1). For all $z \in \partial K$,
\mathcal{T}_K(z) = H_h(z) := \{v \in \mathbb{R}^N : \langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle \geq 0\}.

Proof.

Part 1: $H_h(z) \subseteq \mathcal{T}_K(z)$.

Let $v \in H_h(z)$.

Case 1a: $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle > 0$. By $\mathcal{C}^1$ differentiability,
h(z + tv) = h(z) + t \langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle + o(t) = t \langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle + o(t).


For small $t > 0$, the RHS is $> 0$, hence $z + tv \in K$, and $\operatorname{dist}(z + tv, K) = 0$. Thus $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$.

Case 1b: $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle = 0$. Choose $u \in \mathbb{R}^N$ with $\langle \nabla h(z), u \rangle > 0$; such $u$ exists because $\nabla h(z) \neq 0$. Define $v_n = v + u/n$. Then $\langle \nabla h(z), v_n \rangle = \langle \nabla h(z), u \rangle/n > 0$, so by Case 1a, $v_n \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$. Since $v_n \to v$ and $\mathcal{T}_K(z)$ is closed (as the liminf of continuous functions), $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$.

Part 2: $\mathcal{T}_K(z) \subseteq H_h(z)$.

Let $v \in \mathcal{T}_K(z)$. By definition, there exist sequences $t_n \to 0^+$ and $w_n \in K$ with $t_n^{-1}(w_n - z) \to v$. Set $s_n = |w_n - z|$. Since $h(w_n) \geq 0$ and $h(z) = 0$, by $\mathcal{C}^1$ differentiability:
h(w_n) - h(z) = \langle \nabla h(z), w_n - z \rangle + o(|w_n - z|).


Dividing by $s_n$ (assuming $s_n > 0$; the case $s_n = 0$ is trivial):
\frac{h(w_n) - h(z)}{s_n} = \left\langle \nabla h(z), \frac{w_n - z}{s_n} \right\rangle + \frac{o(s_n)}{s_n}.


The left-hand side is $\geq 0$ because $h(w_n) \geq 0 = h(z)$ and $s_n > 0$. The last term tends to $0$. Hence
\liminf_{n \to \infty} \left\langle \nabla h(z), \frac{w_n - z}{s_n} \right\rangle \geq 0.


Now, $t_n^{-1}(w_n - z) \to v$ and $s_n \to 0$. Write
\frac{w_n - z}{s_n} = \frac{t_n}{s_n} \cdot \frac{w_n - z}{t_n}.


If $t_n / s_n$ is bounded, we may pass to a subsequence with $t_n/s_n \to \lambda \geq 0$, giving $(w_n - z)/s_n \to \lambda v$, and thus $\langle \nabla h(z), \lambda v \rangle \geq 0$, so $\langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle \geq 0$.

If $t_n / s_n \to \infty$, then $(w_n - z)/t_n \to 0$ (since $|w_n - z|/t_n = s_n/t_n \to 0$), contradicting $t_n^{-1}(w_n - z) \to v$ unless $v = 0$, in which case the inequality holds trivially.

Therefore $v \in H_h(z)$. $\square$

D.3 Regularity of the Boundary

Lemma D.2. Under the hypotheses of Lemma D.1, $\partial K$ is a $\mathcal{C}^1$ manifold of dimension $N - 1$.

Proof. This is the regular value theorem applied to $h$ at level $0$: $\partial K = h^{-1}(0)$ and $\nabla h \neq 0$ on $\partial K$. $\square$

Corollary D.3. Under the hypotheses of Lemma D.1, for all $z \in \partial K$,
\mathcal{T}_K(z) = \{v : \langle \nabla h(z), v \rangle \geq 0\},


and $\mathcal{T}_K(z)$ is a closed half-space of dimension $N$.

