Appendix E. Proof of the Main Equivalence Theorem

E.1 Restatement

Theorem E.1 (= Theorem 6.1). Under (A₁)–(A₆) and $u^{\mathrm{crit}} \equiv 0$, the three conditions
\text{(1)} \ \text{Global robust CBF}, \qquad \text{(2)} \ \text{Robust Nagumo–Aubin}, \qquad \text{(3)} \ \text{Boundary dissipation}


satisfy $(1) \implies (2) \iff (3)$.

E.2 Proof of $(1) \implies (3)$

Lemma E.2. If (1) holds with $\alpha \in \mathcal{K}_\infty$, then (3) holds.

Proof. Let $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$. Then $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z) = 0$. Since $\alpha \in \mathcal{K}_\infty$ implies $\alpha(0) = 0$, inequality (5.4) becomes
\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \rangle \geq 0


for all $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$. This is exactly (6.1). $\square$

E.3 Proof of $(3) \iff (2)$ under (A₆)

Lemma E.3. Under (A₆), for all $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$,
F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z) \iff \langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \rangle \geq 0.

Proof. By Corollary D.3 applied to $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ and $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+$,
\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z) = \{v : \langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), v \rangle \geq 0\}.


Substituting $v = F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d)$ yields the equivalence. $\square$

Corollary E.4. Under (A₆), (2) and (3) are equivalent.

E.4 Proof of $(1) \implies (2)$

Proposition E.5. Under (A₁)–(A₆), if (1) holds, then (2) holds.

Proof. By Lemma E.2, (1) implies (3). By Corollary E.4, (3) implies (2). $\square$

E.5 Proof of Corollary 6.2 (Robust Controlled Invariance)

Corollary E.6. Under (A₁)–(A₆), if $\pi$ satisfies (1), then $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is robustly positively invariant:
\forall z_0 \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+,\ \forall d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}},\ \forall t \geq 0, \quad z_M(t; z_0, \pi, d) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+.

Proof. Combine Theorem E.1 with the classical Nagumo–Aubin theorem.

Theorem (Nagumo–Aubin). Let $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ be closed and $F : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^N$ locally Lipschitz. Then $K$ is positively invariant for $\dot{z} = F(z)$ if and only if
F(z) \in \mathcal{T}_K(z) \quad \forall z \in \partial K.

Applying Nagumo–Aubin to $K = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ and $F = F_\sigma(\cdot, \pi(\cdot), d)$ for each fixed $d$, we obtain positive invariance for each trajectory. Since $d$ is arbitrary in $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, invariance holds robustly. $\square$

E.6 Necessity of the CBF Structure

Proposition E.7. Under (A₁)–(A₆), if $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is robustly positively invariant under $\pi$, then (3) holds.

Proof. By Nagumo–Aubin, invariance under each $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ requires $F_\sigma(z, \pi(z), d) \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+}(z)$ for all $z \in \partial \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$. By Corollary D.3, this is equivalent to (3). $\square$

Remark E.8. Proposition E.7 shows that (3) is necessary for invariance, not merely sufficient. Combined with Lemma E.2, this establishes that:
\text{(1)} \implies \text{(3)} \iff \text{(2)} \iff \text{(Robust invariance)}.


The two-sided implication on the right is the content of Nagumo–Aubin; the one-sided implication on the left is the additional content of the CBF framework.

