Appendix G. Buffer Depletion and Finite-Time Collapse

G.1 Setup

Fix $r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}$ and consider the scalar ODE
\dot{S}_M(r,t) = -N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t) + C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) + \rho_M(r, z_M(t), t).

Assumption G.1 (Deficit). There exist $T_0 > 0$ and $\eta > 0$ such that
N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r,t) - C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r,t) - \rho_M(r, z_M(t), t) \geq \eta


for all $t \in [0,T_0]$ along the worst-case trajectory $d^* \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$.

G.2 Finite Depletion Time

Lemma G.2. Under Assumption G.1, if $S_M(r,0) > 0$, then
S_M(r,t) \leq S_M(r,0) - \eta t \quad \forall t \in [0,T_0],


and consequently $S_M(r, T_{\mathrm{dep}}) \leq 0$ for $T_{\mathrm{dep}} = S_M(r,0)/\eta$.

Proof. Integrating the ODE:
S_M(r,t) = S_M(r,0) + \int_0^t \bigl( -N_{\mathrm{requis}} + C_{M,\mathrm{local}} + \rho_M \bigr)(s)\, ds \leq S_M(r,0) - \eta t.


Setting $S_M(r, T_{\mathrm{dep}}) = 0$ gives $T_{\mathrm{dep}} = S_M(r,0)/\eta$. $\square$

G.3 Proof of Theorem 6.3

Theorem G.3 (= Theorem 6.3). Under (A₁)–(A₅), if $D_{\max}(t_0) > \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$ and $T_{\mathrm{closure}} > T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M(t_0))$, then there exists $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ such that
z_M\bigl(t_0 + T_{\mathrm{collapse}}\bigr) \notin \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+, \quad T_{\mathrm{collapse}} \leq T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M(t_0)) < \infty.

Proof. By hypothesis $D_{\max}(t_0) > \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$, so there exists $j \in \mathcal{F}_C$ and $r \in R_j^{\mathrm{ess}}$ with $d_j(t_0) > 0$. This implies
C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r, t_0) + \frac{\tilde{S}_M(r,t_0)}{T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} < N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r, t_0).


Set
\eta := N_{\mathrm{requis}}(r, t_0) - C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(r, t_0) - \frac{\tilde{S}_M(r,t_0)}{T_{\mathrm{rec}}(r)} > 0.


By continuity (Assumption 2.1), there exists a neighborhood $[t_0, t_0 + \delta]$ on which the deficit remains $\geq \eta/2$. Choose $d^* \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$ realizing the worst-case (largest) deficit rate. By Assumption (A₄), $T_{\mathrm{buffer}}(z_M(t_0)) < \infty$. By Lemma G.2 applied to $d^*$, the raw stock $S_M(r,\cdot)$ reaches zero by $T_{\mathrm{dep}} \leq T_{\mathrm{buffer}}$. At that time, $S_M(r, T_{\mathrm{dep}}) = 0$, and since the deficit persists, $S_M(r,t) < 0$ for $t > T_{\mathrm{dep}}$. But $S_M \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|R|}$ by definition of $\mathcal{Z}_M$, so the trajectory leaves $\mathcal{Z}_M$. Since $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_M$, the trajectory also leaves $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$, at a time $T_{\mathrm{collapse}} \leq T_{\mathrm{buffer}}$. $\square$

G.4 Sharpness of the Bound

Example G.4. If $N_{\mathrm{requis}}, C_{M,\mathrm{local}}, \rho_M$ are constant and the deficit $\eta > 0$ is constant, then $T_{\mathrm{collapse}} = T_{\mathrm{buffer}} = S_M(r,0)/\eta$, and the bound is tight.

Example G.5. If $C_{M,\mathrm{local}}(t)$ increases (industrial expansion) and overtakes $N_{\mathrm{requis}}$ before $S_M$ depletes, then $T_{\mathrm{collapse}} = \infty$: the node "escapes" collapse through endogenous capacity growth. This is precisely the closure pathway that the theory aims to certify.

