Appendix I. Hybrid Consistency of the Barrier Function

I.1 Setup

Recall $\mathcal{S}$ is the switching surface. Across $\mathcal{S}$, we impose:

· State continuity: $z(t_k^+) = z(t_k^-)$.
· Barrier consistency: $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z(t_k^+)) \geq h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z(t_k^-))$.

I.2 Trivial Satisfaction Under State Continuity

Lemma I.1. If $z(t_k^+) = z(t_k^-)$, then $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z(t_k^+)) = h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z(t_k^-))$ for any well-defined barrier function $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+$.

Proof. Direct from the definition of a function. $\square$

Corollary I.2. Under state continuity (2.3), the hybrid barrier consistency condition is automatically satisfied.

I.3 Non-Trivial Case: Discontinuous Resets

If the state is allowed to jump (e.g., through a controlled actuation), then the reset map $R : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{Z}_M$ must satisfy
h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(R(z)) \geq h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z) \quad \forall z \in \mathcal{S}.


This is the standard hybrid CBF condition (Ames et al., 2017).

I.4 Mode-Dependent Barrier

If the barrier $h_{\mathrm{via}}^+$ depends on the discrete mode $\sigma$, then the consistency condition becomes
h_{\mathrm{via},\sigma^+}^+(R(z)) \geq h_{\mathrm{via},\sigma^-}^+(z) \quad \forall z \in \mathcal{S}.


Both the state and the barrier must be considered in the reset.

