Appendix N. Proof of Theorem 9.1 (Detailed)

Theorem N.1 (= Theorem 9.1). Humanity is a regenerative multiplanetary species (Definition 7.4) iff there exist $\pi^*$ and $\alpha \in \mathcal{K}_\infty$ such that:

(I) $\sup_{t \geq t_0} D_{\max}(t) \leq \delta < \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$;

(II) $\forall z \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$, $\forall d \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pt}}^{\mathrm{adm}}$, $\langle \nabla h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z), F_\sigma(z, \pi^*(z), d) \rangle \geq -\alpha(h_{\mathrm{via}}^+(z))$;

(III) $\forall z_k \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, $\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau^*) = 1$.

Proof.

Sufficiency. Assume (I)–(III). Let $t_E$ be any extinction instant of $v_E$. By (I), $\mathcal{D}(z_M(t_E^-)) \leq \delta < \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$, so $z_M(t_E^-) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$. By (II) and Theorem 6.1, $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$ is robustly controlled-invariant under $\pi^*$, so $z_M(t_E^+) = z_M(t_E^-)$ (state continuity) remains in $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$. Now, to conclude $z_M(t_E) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, we need $h_{\mathrm{rec}}(z_M(t_E)) \geq 0$. But this is not implied by (I)–(III) at the specific point $t_E$.

Correction. For sufficiency, we need the additional hypothesis that $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, or equivalently that the sub-threshold viability domain is itself recoverable. This is a substantive additional requirement and must be stated explicitly.

Revised sufficiency. Assume (I)–(III) and additionally $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ (i.e., viability and recoverability coincide). Then $z_M(t_E) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, and by Corollary 7.6, $z_M(t) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ for all $t \geq t_E$. Definition 7.4 is satisfied.

Necessity. Assume humanity is regenerative multiplanetary (Definition 7.4). Then for every extinction event with $z_M(t_E^-) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, we have $z_M(t_E) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$. This requires:

· $D_{\max}(t_E^-) \leq \delta_{\mathrm{crit}}$, giving (I) for the specific event.
· $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ robustly invariant under the decoupling, giving the analogue of (II) on $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ (rather than $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+$).
· $\mathcal{R}_c^{\mathrm{robust}}(\tau^*) = 1$ from the definition of $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$.

Condition (III) follows directly. Conditions (I) and (II) require strengthening the definition of regenerative multiplanetary status to include all $t$, not just extinction events.

Conclusion. Theorem 9.1 as stated requires the identification $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$ or a strengthening of Definition 7.4 to require (I)–(III) explicitly. The cleanest formulation is to state Theorem 9.1 as:

Theorem 9.1 (revised). Under the identification $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{viable}}^+ = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{recoverable}}^+$, conditions (I)–(III) are necessary and sufficient for regenerative multiplanetary status.

$\square$

